Response to the team’s update

Verdict: accept the direction, the name and the Tier 2 requirement. I disagree with three points and have tightened four others. None of the changes broadens scope.

Team point	Verdict	What changes
Name: Outcome-Informed Remediation Assurance Triage	Accept	The name is earned only if Tier 2 passes on reviewer-rated usefulness as well as retrieval precision. Otherwise the product ships as Remediation Documentation Alignment Review.
Tier 2 required for the full product	Accept	Tier 1 is still tested first.
Assess the action set, not just actions	Accept, simplify	The model judges each weakness element against each action. Set-level values come from deterministic aggregation. Dependencies are flags, not a dependency graph.
Temporal anchor	Accept, reject one candidate	The default anchor is the final required action completion. A model-derived implementation date is a flag and a sensitivity test, not an anchor, because it puts model error into the date that all retrieval depends on.
Explicit materiality	Accept	Entry routes, with eligibility separated from prioritisation. Queue ordering follows stated rules, not a score.
Separate documentation from remediation quality	Accept, one change	Documentation sufficiency becomes a precondition that gates the mechanism and scope judgements. It is not a further score.
Multidimensional assessment instead of one class	Accept	See below.
Issue formulation mismatch	Research finding only	See below.
Four temporal populations	Accept, make them exclusive	The team’s post-final-action and post-issue-closure periods overlap. Counts would double.
Retain owner fields in a restricted layer	Accept	I add scrubbing of personal names from free text before any embedding or prompt.
Tighter validation	Accept	I add method-independent selection of cases for labelling, with recorded inclusion probabilities.
Failure-specific gates	Accept	Rewritten in section 19.
Value proposition	Confirm	One edit, in section 2.

Why dimensions beat one primary class. Classes overlap, so a forced primary class creates disagreement between labellers that comes from the scheme, not from the record. Each dimension can be checked against specific text. Case-level recommendations then follow from deterministic rules. The cost is more fields per case, which I contain by requiring evidence spans and short allowed values.

Why issue formulation mismatch stays out of the queue. It needs the issue to be wrong and the incident links to be right. Both are uncertain in this data, so a flag could not be validated in the pilot. I capture a labeller flag during Tier 2 review to build the evidence, and revisit it once agreement is known.

Four additions of mine:

The rules baseline gets the same build effort as the model, so it is not a strawman.
An embedding-only retrieval arm isolates what the language model adds.
A small random audit of unflagged eligible cases continues after launch, to track false negatives.
Reviewer outcome codes are captured on every review, which becomes the ongoing ground truth.

Evidence classes keep the earlier two layers. Executive wording: Directly recorded, Inferred and expert-confirmed, Inferred not yet reviewed, Statistically associated, Expert assessment, Causal conclusion not established.

Final analytical specification
1. Name

Outcome-Informed Remediation Assurance Triage (Tier 1 and Tier 2 both validated).
Remediation Documentation Alignment Review (Tier 1 only). This fallback name must be used if Tier 2 fails gate G3.

2. Value proposition

For closed material issues in one pilot domain, identify cases where the recorded remediation may not fully address the recorded weakness, affected scope or verification expectations, and enrich the review with potentially related events observed before and after closure.

It does not decide whether remediation succeeded or failed, whether a control was effective, whether risk rose or fell, whether a later incident was caused by the remediation, or how any person or team performed.

3. Primary user and forum

The Line 2 lead for issue and remediation oversight in the pilot domain, taking the output into the existing closure-challenge or issue-governance forum. This role has independent challenge authority and does not own the actions under review. The forum and a named deputy are confirmed before G0 closes.

4. Primary decision

Which closed material issues warrant proportionate independent review this quarter, and what specifically the reviewer should examine.

5. Eligibility rules for closed material issues

Preconditions (all required):

Status is Closed, not cancelled or withdrawn.
Issue is in the pilot domain scope.
Closed at least 90 days before extraction. This is a proposal, and it ensures that some post-closure window exists.
Not already independently reviewed in the last 12 months, if a review field exists.
Not an umbrella or programme issue. These are excluded and listed separately, detected by many linked actions, multi-mechanism text and programme wording.
An issue closed with no action records stays eligible and is flagged “no action set recorded”.

Entry routes (any one qualifies):

Route	Fields	Note
R1	Current rating High or above	Threshold set by policy owner
R2	Original rating High or above, downgraded before closure	The downgrade is itself recorded as a review reason
R3	Linked to a Severe or Very High incident	Link checked on the labelled sample
R4	Linked incidents with financial or customer impact above sponsor-set thresholds	Thresholds are policy, not model outputs
R5	Audit or regulatory source	
R6	Control-gap indicator set, gap detail populated, rating Medium or above	Indicator precision tested in labelling
R7	SOX or other designated classification	Where applicable in the domain

Eligibility fields: status, domain, current and original rating, source type, control-gap indicator, incident impact ratings and amounts, classification flags, closure date.

Prioritisation-only fields: action count, text length, number of review reasons, time since closure, post-closure signal strength, linked incident count (tie-break only), scope breadth.

Queue ordering uses stated rules agreed with the Line 2 lead before launch, not a score. Cases are ordered by strongest review reason in this proposed order:

Strong post-closure signal involving a Severe or Very High event.
Mechanism misaligned.
Closed before required actions completed.
Scope partial.
Implementation not evidenced.
Verification not evidenced.

Ties are broken by entry route, then closure recency. The queue length is set by the reviewer’s capacity. Each quarter, a random 5% of eligible unflagged cases is reviewed to estimate misses.

6. Remediation alignment case

One case contains:

One issue with its dates, rating history, claimed root cause and control-gap detail.
The complete linked action set, including cancelled and superseded actions.
Per action: status history, creation, due and revised due dates, completion date, cancellation date and reason, description and closure evidence.
Issue closure evidence.
Scope entities from the issue and from the actions: process (with hierarchy descendants), application, product mentions, channel.
Four incident period sets (section 8) and process-level reporting indicators.
Lineage: model, prompt and data snapshot versions.
7. Action-set handling
Weakness elements (model). Break the issue into up to five elements, each typed as mechanism or scope, with the source span. The claimed root cause is recorded as a claim, not as truth.
Action claims (model). For each action, extract what changed, where, what it says about implementation and what it says about verification, with spans. Status and dates are read deterministically.
Element mapping (model). Map each action to each element as addresses, partially addresses, addresses a different mechanism, or unclear, with spans.
Aggregation (deterministic):
Mechanism: Aligned if every mechanism element is addressed by at least one completed action. Partially aligned if some are. Misaligned if none are and at least one action maps to a different mechanism. Insufficient if documentation is insufficient or all mappings are unclear.
Scope: Complete if the union of claimed scope from completed actions covers every issue scope entity. Partial if some. Unclear if scope claims are absent. Not applicable if the issue has a single-process scope.
Cancelled and open actions do not count towards coverage. They are listed.
Sequencing flags (deterministic where possible): action text citing a prerequisite, completion order contradicting a stated prerequisite, issue closed before all actions completed, actions completed after closure, actions added or cancelled in the 30 days before closure, all actions cancelled, a single action covering a multi-element issue.
Output as an element by action matrix.

A narrow action in a well-covered set is therefore not penalised, and a set of individually plausible actions can still show a gap.

8. Temporal model

Dates held:

Issue: identification, creation, original due, each revised due, closure, rating history.
Action: creation, due, revised due, completion, cancellation date.
Incident: occurrence, identification, logging.

Derived: the final required action completion (FRAC) is the latest completion date among non-cancelled actions. If no “required” flag exists, all non-cancelled actions are treated as required.

Default anchor (A0): FRAC.

Bedding-in: events in the first 30 days after A0 are listed separately and excluded from the post-signal count. This is a proposal, with sensitivity runs at 0 and 90 days.

Periods (mutually exclusive):

Period	Definition
P1 pre-identification	12 months before the identification date
P2 during remediation	identification date to A0 (includes any period after closure while actions remained open)
P3 post-final-action, pre-closure	A0 to issue closure, empty if closure is not later than A0
P4 post-closure	the later of A0 and closure, onward

The post-remediation window is 12 months from A0 plus bedding-in, covering P3 and P4. It is compared against the 12-month P1 window, with sensitivity at 6 and 24 months. Windows are truncated at the extract date. Tier 2 is “not assessed” if less than 6 months of post window exists.

Exceptions:

Situation	Handling
Issue closed before FRAC	Anchor stays FRAC. Raise the “closed before actions completed” review reason.
Actions completed after closure	Same, and the later completion is the anchor.
Some actions cancelled	Excluded from FRAC and listed with reasons.
All actions cancelled, or completion dates missing	Fall back to the closure date, label “fallback anchor”, and cap Tier 2 at “possible recurrence”.
Conflicting dates (completion before action creation, closure before identification)	Tier 2 “not assessed” and raise the “date inconsistency” review reason.
Action set changed near closure	Flag only. Anchor unchanged.
Text implies future or phased implementation	Flag, and run a sensitivity analysis at the text-implied date. Never the default anchor.
9. Assessment dimensions
Dimension	Allowed values	Basis
D0 Documentation sufficiency (precondition)	Sufficient, Partial, Insufficient	Model, with rule checks. If Insufficient, D1 and D2 become Insufficient evidence.
D1 Mechanism alignment	Aligned, Partially aligned, Misaligned, Insufficient evidence	Model element mapping, rule aggregation
D2 Scope coverage	Complete, Partial, Unclear, Not applicable	As above
D3 Implementation evidence	Evidenced, Partially evidenced, Not evidenced in the record	Model, with rule checks
D4 Verification evidence	Evidenced, Partially evidenced, Not evidenced in the record	As above
D5 Temporal consistency	Consistent, Conflicting, Unclear (with sub-flags)	Deterministic
D6 Post-closure outcome signal	Not assessed, None retrieved, Possible recurrence, Strong review signal	Retrieval, model relevance grading, deterministic periods

Review reasons are multi-label: mechanism coverage, remediation scope, implementation evidence, verification evidence, post-closure outcomes, date inconsistency, closed before actions completed, rating downgraded before closure, no action set recorded.

Review recommendation: Review, Do not prioritise this quarter, Insufficient basis.

Lead reason is used for reviewer routing only and is not scored for agreement.

D6 proposed definitions. Possible recurrence means at least one “likely” relevant incident in the post window, in scope. Strong review signal means at least two “likely” incidents, or one Severe or Very High “likely” incident, in scope and not in bedding-in. No definition uses a rate.

10. Tier 1 evidence requirements
Issue description, claimed root cause and hierarchy, control-gap indicator and detail, issue closure summary.
Action descriptions and closure summaries, status, dates, cancellation reasons.
Issue-to-action links, with link accuracy verified on the labelled sample.
Scope references for issue and actions.
Minimum text populated and usable in at least 80% of eligible cases (G0).
11. Tier 2 evidence requirements
Incident description, cause, root cause hierarchy, impact ratings, financial impact, occurrence, identification and logging dates, process and application references.
At least 12 months of pre-identification history and at least 6 months of post-anchor observation in the domain, with no source system gap across the windows.
Process-level reporting indicators: median lag from occurrence to logging, share of incidents logged as No Impact or Low, logging volume trend.
Existing issue-to-incident links, used as one signal.
Pools: the primary pool is incidents within issue scope through process hierarchy and application references. A secondary pool of domain-wide text-only candidates is built for recall measurement only and is not shown to reviewers in the MVP. This also gives an early read on scope expansion without making it a product feature.
12. Rules baseline (B1)

Built by an analyst on the same development set, with the same two-week time box as the model effort.

Empty or short closure text, with the threshold set from the labelled distribution.
No verification vocabulary in closure text, with the term list tuned on development data.
Zero actions, or all actions cancelled.
Closure before FRAC, completion after closure, or date conflicts.
Keyword or TF-IDF overlap between issue and action text below a tuned threshold, as the mechanism proxy.
Issue scope entities absent from action text, as the scope proxy.
Action category inconsistent with the issue root cause hierarchy.
Generic-only action types (training, reminder, procedure update).

Output uses the same dimensions with simplified three-value scales.

13. Retrieval baselines
B2 taxonomy: same root cause hierarchy node plus scope.
B3 keyword: BM25 on weakness text with a scope filter.
B4 cross-reference: recorded issue-to-incident links.
B5 current process: reviewers’ own shortlist and search using current tools, same time box.
B6 embedding-only: scope filter plus embedding similarity, without model relevance grading. This isolates the language model’s contribution.

Proposed method: scope filter, then hybrid candidate generation (keyword plus embeddings) over incident description and cause, then model relevance grading against the weakness mechanism and scope, then deterministic period assignment.

14. LLM tasks
Task	Basis
Extract weakness elements and action claims	Language reasoning. One task per call, spans required, fixed settings, versioned prompts.
Map actions to elements	Language reasoning
Judge D0, D3, D4	Language reasoning, backed by rule checks
Grade relevance of retrieved incidents to mechanism and scope	Language reasoning
Eligibility, date arithmetic, anchor, periods, status and cancellation handling, duplicate detection, window counts, flags, aggregation to D1, D2, D5, D6, queue ordering	Deterministic
Entity matching on process and application identifiers	Deterministic
Final labels, adjudication, review recommendation, relevance confirmation, thresholds and route definitions, rule approval, any reversal of a model output	Expert judgement

The model never makes the combined “is this remediation adequate” call.

15. Output schema
case {
  case_id, extract_date, pilot_domain
  eligibility { routes[], excluded_flags[] }
  weakness { elements[{id, type, statement, spans[]}],
             claimed_root_cause {text, spans[]} }
  actions[{ action_id, status, created, due, revised_due[], completed,
            cancelled_date, cancel_reason,
            claims {change, scope, implementation, verification, spans[]},
            element_map[{element_id, relation, spans[]}] }]
  dimensions { D0..D6: {value, basis(rule|model|expert), evidence_class,
                        confidence, spans[], unknowns[]} }
  temporal { dates{}, anchor{date, rule, fallback}, flags[],
             periods{P1..P4: {start, end, count, incident_ids[]}},
             bedding_in{days, incident_ids[]} }
  tier2 { pools{in_scope, domain_check_not_shown},
          matches[{incident_id, period, relevance(likely|possible),
                   mechanism_statement, scope_overlap, spans[],
