Reviewer outcome codes: gap confirmed (mechanism), gap confirmed (scope), gap confirmed (implementation evidence), gap confirmed (verification), no gap found, already known to reviewer, cannot determine.

16. Human-labelling guide
Label only from the recorded text. Do not use outside knowledge or incident data for Tier 1 dimensions.
“Not evidenced in the record” means the record does not say it. It never means “not done”.
Procedure: (1) read the issue and list the weakness elements in your own words, maximum five; (2) read each action and map it to elements; (3) assign D0 to D4; (4) check dates for D5; (5) give a review recommendation and reasons; (6) cite a span for every judgement.
Order: Tier 1 labels are completed before any Tier 2 material is seen. Mechanism and scope are labelled in separate passes to limit one judgement anchoring the other.
Tier 2: pooled incidents are shown without method names and judged as not relevant, possibly relevant or likely relevant to the same mechanism in a relevant scope. Labellers also tick whether the incident suggests the issue described a different mechanism (feeds the research-only signal).
Time and confidence: time-box each case to 10 minutes, record time taken and confidence.
Adjudication: a third labeller sees both labels and spans and decides. Record whether the disagreement came from guide ambiguity or record ambiguity.
Calibration: label 40 cases to refine the guide before any counted labelling. These are not used in metrics.
17. Sampling and test-set design

Frame: all eligible closed issues in the pilot domain across the full history, after deduplication and umbrella exclusion.

Strata: closure era (to reflect changes in field definitions and closure requirements), rating, entry route, closure-text length tercile, action count band (0, 1, 2 to 3, 4 or more).

Selection without bias toward the model:

S1: a stratified random sample of about 150 cases, used for prevalence and unbiased estimates.
S2: about 250 cases for rare conditions, selected by method-independent selectors (each rule flag, keyword and taxonomy flags, cancelled actions, date conflicts) plus random fill.
Model scores are never used to choose labelled cases. Every case carries its inclusion probability, and estimates are re-weighted.

Splits (temporal): development on the oldest closures, calibration in the middle, locked test on the most recent. A separate transfer check applies the older-tuned model to newer closures without retuning, and reports results by era. The test set is opened once.

Sizes (planning assumptions to recompute after the first 100 counted labels reveal agreement and prevalence):

About 400 double-labelled cases, with about 30 examples per retained rare condition.
Tier 2: about 60 issues, top 15 from each method, pooled and deduplicated to about 30 incidents per issue. That is roughly 1,800 judgements, about 60 SME hours at 2 minutes each, with a second labeller on a 30% subset. If effort is too high, reduce pool depth before reducing issues.

Review arm design (for B5): reviewers work matched case batches in both arms in a crossover: current process with a fixed time box, and AI-assisted with the same box. Order is alternated across reviewers.

Post-lock review: SMEs review the top of the model’s queue plus a random sample of unflagged cases, using cases outside the locked test set.

18. Evaluation metrics
Agreement: per dimension (Krippendorff alpha or weighted kappa), per case-level review recommendation, per action and per action set, reported separately.
Model against adjudicated labels: per-class precision and recall with bootstrap intervals, macro-F1, calibration of stated confidence, re-weighted for sampling design.
Ablations: rules against model, with and without Tier 2, embedding-only against full retrieval.
Queue: precision at 30 as judged by the reviewer, recall against seeded known cases, miss rate in the unflagged random sample.
Retrieval: pooled precision at k, estimated recall against the pool and the domain-wide check, relevance-weighted ranking quality, reviewer-rated usefulness of retrieved context.
Stability: all of the above by era and on the locked test set.
Decision usefulness: reviewer time per case, confirmed gaps not found by B5, distinct review actions named, share of completed reviews with a gap confirmed, evidence traceability (share of claims whose span supports them, sampled), reviewer burden (short post-batch rating).
19. Gates and consequences

Thresholds are proposals to agree with model risk before results are seen.

Gate	Pass condition	If missed
G0 Data readiness (privacy and model risk approvals in place)	Usable issue and action narrative in at least 80% of eligible cases. Valid closure dates in at least 90%. Issue-action link or explicit no-action record in at least 80%. Usable incident narrative in at least 80%. At least 6 months post-window for at least 50%.	One extraction or cleansing cycle, or switch to another candidate domain. A second miss stops the work, and the data findings go to the data owners.
G1 Labelling feasibility	Alpha or kappa at least 0.6 per dimension (0.7 for D3 and D4), and at least 0.6 on the review recommendation.	0.4 to 0.6: one guide redefinition. Below 0.4 after redefinition: drop that dimension and ship without it. If D1 and D2 drop but recommendation agreement holds: move to a simpler review-risk classification. If recommendation agreement is also below 0.4: stop.
G2 Tier 1 performance	Model at least 80% of expert-to-expert agreement on each retained dimension, and macro-F1 at least 0.15 above B1 on D1 and D2. The AI-assisted arm finds at least 20% more reviewer-confirmed cases at equal or better precision than B5, or takes half the time at equal confirmed cases.	Rules match the model: drop the language model and deploy rules if G4 holds. Both fail against B5: stop the Tier 1 product. Two tuning cycles on development data only.
G3 Tier 2 retrieval	At least 25% more confirmed relevant incidents at the pooled depth than the best baseline at equal or better precision, on the locked set, and at least 60% of reviewers rate the context as helping the decision.	Retrieval fails: deploy Remediation Documentation Alignment Review only, with no outcome-informed claim. One retry changing candidate generation, not test thresholds. If embedding-only matches the full method, drop the model grading step.
G4 Decision yield (live pilot cycle)	At least 50% of the top 30 judged worth review. At least 5 confirmed gaps not surfaced by B5. A distinct review action named for at least 70% of confirmed cases.	Low yield: keep as analytics only. Surfaces only known cases but cuts review time by at least half: continue as an efficiency tool. Otherwise stop.
G5 Adoption	Named reviewer commits to use the queue in an existing forum for two consecutive cycles and log decisions.	Treated as an operating-model failure, not analytical invalidity. Preserve the artefacts, reopen once with a different sponsor or forum, and close after two quarters.

Failure type to consequence:

Failure	Consequence
Analytical proposition (experts cannot agree on review need)	Stop.
Language model component	Reject it, retain rules if they change decisions.
Retrieval component	Retain Documentation Alignment Review, no outcome claim.
Data quality	One repair, then change domain or stop.
Organisational adoption	Hold artefacts, change sponsor or forum once.
Experts disagree on mechanism but agree on review need	Simpler review-risk classification.
